---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Chat from '../components/Chat.jsx'
import { chapters } from '../data/chapters.js'

// chapters that are written so far get links
const written = new Set(Object.keys(import.meta.glob('./*.mdx')).map(p => p.slice(2, -4)))
const first = chapters.find(c => written.has(c.slug))

const flow = [
  ['Data', 'FineWeb-Edu and smol-smoltalk come as Parquet files, read by my own Parquet, snappy and zstd code.'],
  ['Tokenizer', 'It is byte-level BPE with 32,768 tokens, trained on a sample and then run over billions of tokens.'],
  ['Training', 'The forward pass, the backward pass and AdamW run in PTX on an RTX 5070 Ti.'],
  ['Quantization', 'The trained weights are shrunk to int8 or int4.'],
  ['Chat', 'One engine runs on the CPU with AVX2 and VNNI, and a second one in WebAssembly runs in the browser.'],
]
---
<Base>
  <div class="split">
    <div class="main">
      <h1>A chat model I wrote in assembly</h1>
      <p>
        Mnemonic is a small language model that I wrote by hand in x86-64 assembly and in PTX, the assembly language
        of NVIDIA's GPUs. That covers everything it took to make it: reading the training data, the tokenizer, the
        training on the GPU, shrinking the weights afterwards, and the program you chat with. None of it is C or
        Python, and it doesn't use the CUDA toolkit. The only outside code it calls is the Windows API and the NVIDIA
        driver.
      </p>
      <p>
        I trained it at home on an RTX 5070 Ti, which is a graphics card made for games. It's nowhere near the models
        people usually chat with, but it knows its name, keeps up a simple conversation and explains everyday things
        reasonably well.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can try it further down this page. I built it in eleven stages and wrote a chapter about each one, starting
        with {first ? <a href={`/${first.slug}`}>{first.title.toLowerCase()}</a> : 'the chapters below'}.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="side">
      <p class="side-head">The current version</p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Parameters</dt>
        <dd>304 million, in 24 layers</dd>
        <dt>Pretraining</dt>
        <dd>10 billion tokens of FineWeb-Edu in 90.4 hours</dd>
        <dt>Chat tuning</dt>
        <dd>389 million tokens in 3.4 hours</dd>
        <dt>Training speed</dt>
        <dd>about 32,000 tokens a second, 68 TFLOPS</dd>
        <dt>GPU</dt>
        <dd>one RTX 5070 Ti</dd>
        <dt>CPU chat</dt>
        <dd>about 220 tokens a second, int8, 12 threads of a Core Ultra 7 265F</dd>
        <dt>Browser engine</dt>
        <dd>9,867 bytes of WebAssembly</dd>
      </dl>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <figure class="flow-fig">
    <ol class="flow">
      {flow.map(([name, what]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="step">{name}</span>
          <span class="what">{what}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <figcaption>From the raw data to a chat. Every step on this line is assembly I wrote.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="split" id="try">
    <div class="main">
      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p>
        It runs in your browser on your own computer, so the model has to download before it can answer, and nothing
        downloads until you press the button. It handles small talk and simple explanations, but it often gets facts
        and arithmetic wrong, and it does better with one question at a time than with a long back and forth.
      </p>
      <Chat client:load />
    </div>
    <aside class="side note-col">
      <p>
        Nothing you type leaves your computer. The engine is the hand-written WebAssembly from the last chapter, and
        the page starts one thread for each core, all sharing the model in one block of memory.
      </p>
      <p>The int8 models give better answers. The int4 ones are smaller and a little faster.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="chapters">
    <h2>How it was built</h2>
    <ul class="toc">
      {chapters.map(c => (
        <li>
          <span class="t">{written.has(c.slug) ? <a href={`/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a> : <>{c.title} [TODO]</>}</span>
          <span class="what">{c.summary}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="main outro">
    <p>If you'd rather run it on your own machine, Ollama has the 300M:</p>
    <pre><code>ollama run supergoatscriptguy/mnemonic</code></pre>
    <p>
      The weights for its own CPU engine are on <a href="https://huggingface.co/SuperGoatScriptGuy/Mnemonic">Hugging Face</a>,
      and the code is on <a href="https://github.com/Supergoatscriptguy/Mnemonic">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--main)) var(--side);
    column-gap: var(--gap);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .side {
    font: 15px/1.55 var(--sans);
    color: var(--muted);
    padding-top: 0.6rem;
  }
  .side-head {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    margin: 0;
    font-variant-numeric: lining-nums;
  }
  .facts dt,
  .facts dd {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 6px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .facts dt {
    padding-right: 14px;
  }
  .facts dd {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  #try .side {
    padding-top: 4.4rem;
  }
  .flow-fig {
    margin: 4.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .flow {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    gap: 28px;
    font: 15px/1.5 var(--sans);
  }
  .flow li {
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: 12px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--text);
  }
  .flow .step {
    display: block;
    font: 600 1.05rem var(--serif);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .flow .what {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  #try {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }
  #chapters {
    margin-top: 4.5rem;
  }
  .toc {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .toc li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr;
    column-gap: 2rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .toc li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .toc .what {
    color: var(--muted);
    font: 16px/1.5 var(--sans);
    padding-top: 2px;
  }
  .outro {
    margin-top: 4rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 999px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .side {
      margin-top: 1.2rem;
    }
    #try .side {
      padding-top: 0;
    }
    .flow {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 18px;
    }
    .toc li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
